{{also|Riddle}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=riddle (disambiguation)|mul=Riddle}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɪdəl}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|redel|lang=enm}}, {{term|redels|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|rǣdels}}, {{m|ang|rǣdelse||counsel", "opinion", "imagination", "riddle}}, from Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*rēdisliją||counsel, conjecture}}. Akin to {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|rādisli}} ({{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|raadsel|lang=nl}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|rādisle}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Rätsel||riddle|lang=de}}), {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|rǣdan||to read, advise, interpret}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[verbal]] [[puzzle]], [[mystery]], or other [[problem]] of an [[intellectual]] nature.
#: {{usex|lang=en|"Here's a '''riddle''': It's black, and white, and red all over. What is it?"}}
#* {{w|John Milton}} (1608-1674)
#*: To wring from me, and tell to them, my secret, / That solved the '''riddle''' which I had proposed.
#*{{quote-book|year=1907|author={{w|Robert W. Chambers|Robert Chambers}}
|title=[http://gutenberg.org/ebooks/14852 The Younger Set]|chapter=8
|passage=“I never understood it,” she observed, lightly scornful. “What occult meaning has a sun-dial for a spooney ?  ''I’m'' sure I don't want to read '''riddles''' into a strange gentleman’s optics.”}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|verbal puzzle, mystery or problem}} [[enigma]], [[conundrum]], [[brain-teaser]]

