===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|riddil|lang=enm}}, {{term|ridelle||sieve|lang=enm}} from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|hriddel||sieve|lang=ang}}, alteration of earlier {{term|hridder|lang=ang}}, {{m|ang|hrīder}} from Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*hridą||sieve}}, from Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*hrid-||to shake}}, from Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*krey-}}. Akin to {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Reiter||sieve|lang=de}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|hreinn||pure, clean|lang=non}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|hreini||pure, clean|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃||tr=hrains|clean, pure|lang=got}}. More at {{l|en|rinse}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[sieve]] with coarse [[mesh]]es, usually of wire, for separating coarser materials from finer, as chaff from grain, cinders from ashes, or gravel from sand.
# A [[board]] with a row of [[pin]]s, set [[zigzag]], between which wire is drawn to [[straighten]] it.

